      "filter_fp": 0,
      "filter_fn": 0,
      "filter_errors": [],
      "answer": "Manhua is the term for Chinese comics, written in Chinese characters and published in a variety of formats.  In mainland China the text is laid out horizontally and read from left to right, while in Taiwan and Hong Kong it is usually written vertically top\u2011to\u2011bottom and read from right to left, mirroring the style of Japanese manga.  Many manhua are released online as web manhua, which are popular for their ease of upload, color printing and free access.\n\nManhwa, on the other hand, is the Korean word for comics.  It is generally read horizontally from left to right, though it can also be presented vertically.  Manhwa has its own distinct visual and narrative style, heavily influenced by Japanese manga but differentiated from both manga and manhua.\n\nThus, the main differences are origin (China vs. Korea), language (Chinese characters vs. Korean), typical reading direction (variable in manhua, usually left\u2011to\u2011right in manhwa), and stylistic conventions that set each medium apart.",
